{
  "image_name": "1f8699985e462095509fa828b238cc93.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how gut microbes and the intestinal barrier shape immune responses that can spread beyond the intestine. At the top, diverse bacteria occupy the gut lumen above a mucus layer and epithelial cells. Several routes of microbial sensing are shown: CX3CR1-positive dendritic cells sample material from the lumen, goblet cells pass bacterial antigens to CD103-positive dendritic cells, and M cells take up antigen. These antigen-presenting pathways drive T-cell differentiation, especially T helper 17 (TH17) cells, but also regulatory T cells, with the balance influenced by microbial metabolites such as short-chain fatty acids and indoles.\n\nSegmented filamentous bacteria (SFB) are highlighted as promoting recruitment of homeostatic α4β7-positive TH17 and TFH17-related cells. In lymphoid tissue, TFH17 cells interact with B cells and germinal centers, involving MHCII, TCR, CD40/CD40L, and S1P/S1PR1 signaling, before trafficking systemically. The lower part shows immune cells entering blood, emphasizing movement from the gut to extra-intestinal sites.\n\nOverall, the figure presents the gut as an immune-education site where microbial antigens and metabolites tune the TH17 versus Treg response. It also suggests that in rheumatoid arthritis and spondyloarthritis, this balance appears shifted toward TH17-type cells, potentially contributing to disease.",
